  "summary": "The country's architecture pays further establishment testament to Britain, with many historic buildings still standing.",
  "description": "Architecture: The country's architecture pays further establishment testament to Britain, with many historic buildings still standing. In addition to traditional wood and stone, coral was also used in construction, lending a unique Barbadian flair. Jacobean, Georgian and Victorian styles dominate. But slaves constructed many of these buildings, as well as their own chattel houses, so they were an integral part of the island's architectural legacy. Built of wood, chattel houses were set atop blocks instead of permanent foundations so they could be easily moved from place to place. The vivid colours of these chattel houses show the West African influence.",
  "culturalContext": "Compiled from the “Architecture” section of Wikipedia's “Culture of Barbados”.",
